Ref2D against #106 over the grid: 120 of 120 exits at the same second and price; the lenders' P&L
within 0.07 points of Q's value, the borrower's share within 0.11 points of V (#106's
reference cost is the live pool's, so the penalty base min(B_live, B_ref) and the lenders' penalty on it are
a little higher where a pulled deposit left L_slow above the pool).
A pulled deposit changes the control's outcome in 12 of the 80 deposit cells: open -> liquidation at 2x / 1 s, 2x / 30 s, 2x / 120 s, 2x / 300 s (3x the pool, sizes 1 / 0.5); liquidation -> S1 at 3x / 1 s (1x / 3x the pool, sizes 1 / 0.5).
Sizing is on D under #106, but the margin is priced at the open on the pool as it is (max(B_spot, B_ema) at
the deposited depth), so a position opened while the deposit is held posts a little less margin than the same
q on D.  Tier C's opening CR of 300 % puts the liquidation threshold at about 2x and the V/B edge at about 3x;
the thinner margin moves both a little lower: a hold at 2.00x liquidates (the control stays open) and a 3x gap
that liquidates the control with the borrower zeroed settles in S1, the lenders whole in both.  Past the edge
every row, the control's too, is the genuine gap class (S1), and the pulled deposit adds 0.3-3.5 points of
Q to the lenders' loss.

