Why the cap is D (M6E-1).  With the positions sized on D and pool_lock_min_bps at its default, a cap at 2D
changes no liquidation in (i)-(iii): a buyback on the drained pool costs (1 - c/2)/(1 - c) - 1 more than a
reference at twice its depth, 1.27 % at the impact cap's c = 2.47 %, inside delta_liq = 2 %.  It is outside
delta_exp = 1 % above c = 1.96 % (about 80 % of the cap): (iv) at 2D a deposit held into a deadline and pulled
there holds such an expiry up for as long as L_slow stays above twice the drained depth -- up to an hour, a
free extension for the borrower with the lenders' tokens out, no attacker-only second needed (a genuine LP
leaving at the deadline does the same); and (v) at a governed pool_lock_min_bps below 5,000 the cap
floor(D*1e4/lambda_min) is 3D-5D and the pulled deposit gaps a position sized on D into S1 again, M6D-1's
loss.  At D the reference is the live pool (D <= L_live): no third-party deposit is credited to an exit, as to
a size or a tier, and pool_lock_min_bps reaches no PumpSwap term.  What the EMA terms gave on PumpSwap -- a
buyback wrapped in the LP's own withdrawal priced against the deeper pool -- is (iii)'s difference: the same
second, the borrower's share of V at most 0.1 points lower, the lenders whole.

